  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 1238 PM MDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nRich, low level air remained in place across the plains this\nafternoon. This along with daytime heating is building MLCAPE \ninto the 1000-1800 J/kg range. Local ACARS soundings still show a \npretty healthy cap in place, but we are warming and should at \nleast get close to convective temperatures. The most likely area \nfor convective initiation of course is over the mountains (already\nongoing), but also the higher terrain of the Palmer Divide and \nCheyenne Ridge. Storms will likely move in a southeasterly \ndirection once they develop, and a weakening cap is expected to \nallow propagation across the plains and portions of the I-25 \nCorridor. Bulk shear will be increasing to 30-35 kts, so enough \nshear and instability is present for a few supercells. Thus, large\nhail and damaging winds will be a threat with any storms through \nmid evening. It should be worth noting that we'll be under the \nright entrance region of an upper level speed max, so weak forcing\nshould help coverage a bit so we did bump pops up ever so \nslightly, with the highest odds of storms over the northeast \nplains and Palmer Divide. That upper level speed max could also \naid storms to last a bit longer into the evening than normal. \n\nWe still expect clearing skies overnight, and enough lingering low\nlevel moisture for some patchy fog over the plains and in the \nhigh mountain valleys. \n\nMonday will feature warmer and drier weather as the ridge axis\nretrogrades to the west-northwest. That puts our forecast into a\ndrier westerly flow aloft, and mid level temperatures will also be\nwarming. Highs are expected to reach the upper 80s to lower 90s\nover the plains and I-25 Corridor. We'll still likely have enough\nlingering moisture and strong daytime heating to support isolated\nlate day convection. The airmass along the I-25 Corridor would be\nmore supportive of just isolated late afternoon/evening sprinkles\nand microbursts as DCAPE grows to ~1500 J/kg. Similar weather\nconditions although further warming of a few degrees is expected\nfor Tuesday. Also, a late afternoon frontal passage will likely \nbe enough to support growing high based shower/storm coverage into\nthe Tuesday evening and overnight hours. \n\nWednesday is still on track to be slightly cooler with increased\nchances of storms again as the post frontal airmass will be cooler\nand more moist. Average MLCAPE from various models suggest\n1000-2000 J/kg, while bulk shear is an impressive 40-50 kts so a\ngrowing severe storm threat will be in the picture. \n\nThursday and Friday are shaping up to be drier again as the ridge\namplifies, this time across Colorado and eastern Utah. That means\na stronger thermal ridge and high temperatures pushing well above\nnormal...into the lower to mid 90s across the plains and I-25 \nCorridor. While the ridge should hold fairly steady across the \nCentral Rockies through at least next weekend, ensembles have good\nagreement for an increase in monsoonal moisture under the ridge. \nThat would support a return to scattered afternoon and evening \nshower/storms as well, with the greatest chances holding over the \nmountains at least until early next week.",
